# Webhook Payloads

When an event occurs, Teamtailor sends a JSON payload to your endpoint containing details about the event.

## Payload Structure

```json
{
  "payload": {
    "event_name": "job.update",
    "data": {
      "id": "2",
      "type": "jobs",
      "attributes": {
        "title": "IT Designer 1",
        "body": "<p>Job description...</p>",
        "company-name": "Teamtailor",
        "cover-letter-requirement": "cover_letter_optional",
        "created-at": "2023-02-08T10:10:13.611+01:00",
        "employment-level": "none",
        "employment-type": "none",
        "human-status": "archived",
        "internal": false,
        "remote-status": "none",
        "status": "archived",
        "tags": [],
        "updated-at": "2023-02-22T22:10:48.705+01:00"
      }
    }
  },
  "signature": "YzU1N2FhOTUwMjlkNTFiMGM5NjIxNTEyODc5NGY5ZjgxZWNkMmNkZTZhNmIxYmI2YmM3NmVmYmQ1ZGZiMDg0Zg=="
}
```

The standard payload structure includes:

1. `payload`: Object containing:
   - `event_name`: The type of event (e.g., "job.update")
   - `data`: Resource data with:
     - `id`: Resource identifier
     - `type`: Resource type (e.g., "jobs", "candidates")
     - `attributes`: Resource-specific attributes
2. `signature`: Base64-encoded signature for verification

For `destroy` events, the data structure is simpler but follows the same overall structure.

## Resource-Specific Attributes

Each resource type includes different attributes in the payload:

### Candidate
- first_name, last_name, email, phone etc.
- tags, custom fields, status

### Job
- title, body, status, company-name etc.
- employment details, dates, tags

### Job Application
The job application resource includes the following attributes:

- `id` - Application identifier
- `updated_at` - Last update timestamp
- `job_id` - Associated job identifier
- `job_title` - Title of the associated job
- `last_moved_at` - Timestamp when application was last moved between stages
- `stage_name` - Current stage name
- `referring_site` - Site where candidate found the job
- `referring_url` - URL where candidate found the job
- `referer_id` - Referer identifier
- `rejected_at` - Rejection timestamp (if applicable)
- `promotion_id` - Associated promotion identifier
- `company_uuid` - Company unique identifier
- `transparent_recruiting_visible_at` - Earliest date when rejection can be communicated to candidate

<aside class="warning">
<strong>Transparent Recruiting Compliance:</strong> If your system notifies candidates about application status changes (especially rejections), you must respect the <code>transparent_recruiting_visible_at</code> date. This represents the earliest date when you can notify the candidate about their rejection status.
</aside>

### Audit Event
An audit event payload has its attributes directly in `payload`, with snake_case keys:

```json
{
  "payload": {
    "event_name": "audit_event.create",
    "id": 143,
    "action": "update",
    "actor_id": 1,
    "actor_label": "Jane Doe",
    "actor_type": "user",
    "changes": [
      {"field": "title", "label": "Title", "visible": true, "old_and_new": true, "from": "Developer", "to": "Senior developer"},
      {"field": "body", "label": "Body", "visible": false, "old_and_new": true, "from": null, "to": null}
    ],
    "company_uuid": "OF71skEUFrs",
    "description": null,
    "entry_client": "web_app",
    "ip_address": "203.0.113.7",
    "source_id": 42,
    "source_label": "Senior developer",
    "source_type": "job",
    "timestamp": "2026-11-21T15:47:46.573+01:00",
    "teamtailor_host": "teamtailor.com"
  },
  "signature": "NjJmOGIyYTYyNmNmNzVlYjEyZDk4ZmYxODQzYTFhZTlkZDUzNzljM2NlZDEyNzBiNjgwMmFkNDkyOGQ4MmU0YQ=="
}
```

- `id` - Audit event identifier, the same as in `GET /v1/audit-events`
- `action` - What happened, such as `create`, `update` or `destroy`
- `source_type`, `source_id`, `source_label` - The record the event is about, such as a job or a candidate. `source_label` keeps the record's name after the record is deleted
- `actor_type`, `actor_id`, `actor_label` - Who did it. `actor_type` is one of `user`, `api_key`, `copilot`, `mcp`, `candidate` and `system`
- `changes` - The fields that changed. `label` is always in English, so match on `field`. `from` and `to` are `null` for fields whose values the audit log does not show, such as the job ad text. Secrets, such as API key values and passwords, are never included
- `entry_client` - Where the change came from: `web_app`, `mobile_app`, `sourcing_extension`, `career_site`, `public_api`, `mcp`, `copilot`, `partner`, `system` or `unknown`
- `ip_address` - The IP address of the request, or `null` when there was none
- `description` - A short text about the event, or `null`
- `timestamp` - When the event happened
- `company_uuid` - Company unique identifier

Teamtailor sends the webhook after the event is saved. Events can arrive in a different order than they happened, so sort them by `timestamp` or `id`. A failed delivery is not retried. To make sure that no event is missing, also collect new events at a regular interval with `GET /v1/audit-events` (see the Audit Events section of the [API documentation](https://docs.teamtailor.com)), and skip events whose `id` you already have.

## Headers

Each webhook request includes these headers:

- `TT-Signature`: Signature for verifying webhook authenticity
- `Authorization`: Bearer token (for partner webhooks, if applicable)
- `teamtailor-api-token`: API token (if applicable)

<aside class="notice">
Note that attributes follow kebab-case naming convention (e.g., "company-name", "remote-status"). Audit event attributes use snake_case (e.g., "actor_label", "source_type").
</aside>